<div class="proj-content">

  <div class="proj-section">
    <div class="proj-section-title">Overview</div>
    <p>This project involved the complete mechanical design and analysis of a two-stage speed reduction gearbox combining a helical gear first stage and a spur gear second stage. The gearbox was designed to achieve a specified speed reduction ratio while meeting strength, fatigue life, and geometric constraints.</p>
    <p>The design process followed a systematic approach: gear geometry calculation → shaft design under combined bending and torsion → key and coupling design → bearing selection for rated life → housing design → tolerance stacking and fit analysis. Each component was modelled in SolidWorks and validated through analytical calculations and FEA.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="proj-section">
    <div class="proj-section-title">Design Specifications</div>
    <div class="specs-grid">
      <div class="spec-cell"><div class="spec-label">Configuration</div><div class="spec-value">Helical + Spur (2-stage)</div></div>
      <div class="spec-cell"><div class="spec-label">Gear Material</div><div class="spec-value">Alloy steel (case-hardened)</div></div>
      <div class="spec-cell"><div class="spec-label">Shaft Material</div><div class="spec-value">Carbon steel</div></div>
      <div class="spec-cell"><div class="spec-label">Design Standard</div><div class="spec-value">AGMA / IS 2535</div></div>
      <div class="spec-cell"><div class="spec-label">CAD Tool</div><div class="spec-value">SolidWorks</div></div>
      <div class="spec-cell"><div class="spec-label">FEA Tool</div><div class="spec-value">ANSYS Structural</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="proj-section">
    <div class="proj-section-title">Design Process</div>
    <p><strong>Gear design:</strong> Helical and spur gear geometry was calculated from the input speed, output speed, and power requirements. Module, number of teeth, helix angle (for stage 1), face width, and centre distance were determined to satisfy bending strength (Lewis equation) and surface durability (Hertz contact stress) criteria with appropriate safety factors.</p>
    <p><strong>Shaft design:</strong> Input, intermediate, and output shafts were designed for the combined bending moment and torque loading using the ASME shaft design equation. Shaft diameters were sized for both static and fatigue loading, with stress concentration factors at keyways and shoulders applied.</p>
    <p><strong>Bearing selection:</strong> Rolling element bearings were selected from standard catalogues based on the calculated radial and axial loads at each support, targeting a rated L10 life of 10,000 hours or greater.</p>
    <p><strong>FEA validation:</strong> Critical components — gear teeth under maximum tangential load and shaft sections at stress concentrations — were modelled and simulated in ANSYS to verify that maximum von Mises stress remained below allowable limits with appropriate safety margins.</p>
