<div class="proj-page-hero">
  <div class="proj-page-label">Project 05 · Sep 2025 – Dec 2025 · UC San Diego</div>
  <div class="proj-page-title">Wearable Transcranial Magnetic Neuromodulation Cap for Motor Symptom Management in Parkinson's Disease</div>
  <div class="proj-page-meta">
    <div class="proj-page-meta-item">Type: <span>Academic Design Project</span></div>
    <div class="proj-page-meta-item">Institution: <span>UC San Diego</span></div>
    <div class="proj-page-meta-item">Domain: <span>Neurotechnology / Wearables</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class="proj-page-tags">
    <span class="tag">TMS</span><span class="tag">Transcranial Magnetic Stimulation</span><span class="tag">Parkinson's Disease</span><span class="tag">Neurotechnology</span><span class="tag">Wearable</span><span class="tag">Motor Symptoms</span><span class="tag">Non-invasive</span>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="proj-content">

  <div class="proj-section">
    <div class="proj-section-title">Overview</div>
    <p>This project addresses the challenge of motor symptom management in Parkinson's disease (PD) — specifically tremor, rigidity, and bradykinesia — through a wearable transcranial magnetic stimulation (TMS) cap designed for home use. Conventional TMS therapy requires clinical visits to stationary equipment, severely limiting treatment accessibility and frequency for PD patients.</p>
    <p>The proposed device integrates TMS coil elements into a comfortable, head-worn cap form factor, targeting specific cortical motor regions associated with PD motor dysfunction. The goal is to enable non-invasive, repeatable neuromodulation therapy that patients can self-administer, reducing reliance on dopaminergic pharmacotherapy and clinical appointment burden.</p>
    <div class="highlight-box"><p>Parkinson's disease affects over 10 million people worldwide. Motor symptoms significantly impair quality of life, and current pharmacological treatments (levodopa) have dose-limiting side effects. Non-invasive neuromodulation offers a promising complementary approach.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div class="proj-section">
    <div class="proj-section-title">Design Considerations</div>
    <div class="specs-grid">
      <div class="spec-cell"><div class="spec-label">Stimulation Type</div><div class="spec-value">Repetitive TMS (rTMS)</div></div>
      <div class="spec-cell"><div class="spec-label">Target Region</div><div class="spec-value">Primary motor cortex (M1)</div></div>
      <div class="spec-cell"><div class="spec-label">Form Factor</div><div class="spec-value">Head-worn cap</div></div>
      <div class="spec-cell"><div class="spec-label">Use Setting</div><div class="spec-value">Home / ambulatory</div></div>
      <div class="spec-cell"><div class="spec-label">Safety</div><div class="spec-value">Non-invasive, closed-loop dosing</div></div>
      <div class="spec-cell"><div class="spec-label">Symptoms Targeted</div><div class="spec-value">Tremor, rigidity, bradykinesia</div></div>
    </div>
    <p>Key design challenges include miniaturizing the TMS coil driver to wearable size while maintaining sufficient magnetic field strength, ensuring spatial targeting accuracy without neuronavigation infrastructure, and building safety interlocks to prevent overstimulation in an unsupervised home setting.</p>
    <p>The cap uses a distributed coil array approach with modular positioning, allowing the stimulation focus to be adjusted based on the patient's anatomy. A closed-loop dosing protocol monitors session parameters and enforces safety limits automatically.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="proj-section">
    <div class="proj-section-title">Clinical Context</div>
    <p>TMS for Parkinson's disease has been studied extensively in clinical settings, with evidence supporting rTMS over M1 and supplementary motor area (SMA) for reducing motor symptom severity. The primary barrier to adoption is device size and treatment inconvenience. A wearable format could dramatically increase treatment frequency and thus cumulative therapeutic benefit.</p>
    <p>This project focused on the device engineering and human factors design aspects, with reference to existing clinical TMS literature for parameter selection and the regulatory pathway considerations for eventual Class II or Class III device classification.</p>
  </div>

</div>

