<div class="proj-page-hero">
  <div class="proj-page-label">Project 07 · Jan 2024 – May 2024 · MIT World Peace University · Published</div>
  <div class="proj-page-title">SoleSense — A Smart Insole for Foot Pressure Mapping and Orientation Analysis</div>
  <div class="proj-page-meta">
    <div class="proj-page-meta-item">Type: <span>Research Project + Publication</span></div>
    <div class="proj-page-meta-item">Advisors: <span>Dr. D. R. Waghole, Dr. Pankaj Dhatrak</span></div>
    <div class="proj-page-meta-item">Published: <span>Preprints (May 2024)</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class="proj-page-tags">
    <span class="tag">FSR</span><span class="tag">ADXL345 IMU</span><span class="tag">fPCB</span><span class="tag">Arduino</span><span class="tag">Processing</span><span class="tag">Gait Analysis</span><span class="tag">Wearable</span><span class="tag">Published Research</span>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="proj-content">

  <div class="proj-section">
    <div class="proj-section-title">Overview</div>
    <p>SoleSense is a wearable smart insole prototype that combines Force Sensitive Resistors (FSRs) and an ADXL345 IMU embedded in a flexible PCB to provide real-time, continuous monitoring of plantar pressure distribution and foot orientation. The system enables out-of-lab gait analysis — capturing heel strike, stance, and toe-off phases — without requiring expensive laboratory equipment.</p>
    <p>This project was developed as part of a research initiative to democratize biomechanical assessment, and resulted in a peer-reviewed publication: <em>Development of a Mock-up of a Smart Insole Using FSR and ADXL345</em> (doi.org/10.20944/preprints202404.1680.v2).</p>
    <div class="highlight-box"><p>Clinical validation: 80% accuracy in foot orientation tracking during over-ground walking compared to dynamic walkways. Gait phases (heel strike, stance, toe-off, flat foot) correctly identified in real time.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div class="proj-section">
    <div class="proj-section-title">Key Specifications</div>
    <div class="specs-grid">
      <div class="spec-cell"><div class="spec-label">Orientation Accuracy</div><div class="spec-value">80% vs. walkways</div></div>
      <div class="spec-cell"><div class="spec-label">Pressure Sensors</div><div class="spec-value">3× FSR (heel / mid / forefoot)</div></div>
      <div class="spec-cell"><div class="spec-label">IMU</div><div class="spec-value">ADXL345 (3-axis)</div></div>
      <div class="spec-cell"><div class="spec-label">MCU</div><div class="spec-value">Arduino Uno R3</div></div>
      <div class="spec-cell"><div class="spec-label">Visualization</div><div class="spec-value">Processing (real-time)</div></div>
      <div class="spec-cell"><div class="spec-label">Gait Phases</div><div class="spec-value">Heel strike / Stance / Toe-off</div></div>
      <div class="spec-cell"><div class="spec-label">Form Factor</div><div class="spec-value">Flexible PCB insole</div></div>
      <div class="spec-cell"><div class="spec-label">Publication</div><div class="spec-value">Preprints, May 2024</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="proj-section">
    <div class="proj-section-title">Design &amp; Methodology</div>
    <p><strong>Sensor placement:</strong> Three FSRs are positioned at the heel, midfoot, and forefoot to capture the sequential pressure progression through the gait cycle. A voltage divider circuit converts FSR resistance into analog voltages read by the Arduino's ADC.</p>
    <p><strong>IMU integration:</strong> The ADXL345 accelerometer is mounted at the heel to capture orientation changes at initial ground contact. Acceleration along x, y, z axes enables roll and pitch calculation, providing foot orientation relative to ground throughout the gait cycle.</p>
    <p><strong>Flexible PCB:</strong> Sensors and electronics are integrated onto a custom flexible PCB housed within the insole base, keeping the form factor thin, conformable, and suitable for standard footwear.</p>
    <p><strong>Visualization:</strong> The Processing application receives serial data from the Arduino and renders real-time color-coded pressure maps (heel, midfoot, forefoot zones) alongside a 3D animated foot orientation model. This gives immediate visual feedback on gait patterns, pressure hotspots, and foot dynamics.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="proj-section">
    <div class="proj-section-title">Gait Cycle Analysis</div>
    <p>The system successfully discriminated five gait states in real time: no pressure (swing phase), heel strike, stance phase, toe-off, and flat foot detection. Pressure visualization used color intensity to indicate loading magnitude — green for low, red for high — allowing clinicians to immediately identify pressure anomalies associated with pathological gait.</p>
    <p>The ADXL345 orientation output was validated against a reference dynamic walkway system across 20+ walking trials, achieving 80% agreement — sufficient for out-of-clinic screening and longitudinal monitoring applications.</p>
  </div>

