<div class="proj-page-hero">
  <div class="proj-page-label">Project 04 · Sep 2025 – Dec 2025 · UC San Diego</div>
  <div class="proj-page-title">AccuSense Cath — Soft-Tip Cardiac Catheter with Contact-Force Haptic Feedback</div>
  <div class="proj-page-meta">
    <div class="proj-page-meta-item">Type: <span>Academic Project</span></div>
    <div class="proj-page-meta-item">Course: <span>BENG 219 / Capstone</span></div>
    <div class="proj-page-meta-item">Institution: <span>UC San Diego</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class="proj-page-tags">
    <span class="tag">SolidWorks</span><span class="tag">ANSYS</span><span class="tag">Arduino Nano 33 BLE</span><span class="tag">MATLAB</span><span class="tag">CNT Doping</span><span class="tag">Piezoresistive</span><span class="tag">Haptics</span><span class="tag">SLA 3D Print</span><span class="tag">ISO 10993</span>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="proj-content">

  <div class="proj-section">
    <div class="proj-section-title">Overview</div>
    <p>AccuSense Cath is a 6 Fr cardiac ablation catheter prototype designed to address one of the most persistent safety challenges in interventional cardiology: the inability to quantify contact force between the catheter tip and heart tissue in real time. With over 300,000 cardiac ablation procedures performed annually in the US, approximately 15,000 involve complications — many stemming from unintended tissue damage caused by inconsistent catheter force application.</p>
    <p>The device integrates a Carbon Nanotube (CNT)-doped elastomeric distal tip that functions as a piezoresistive force sensor, combined with an IMU for contact angle and velocity tracking. Together, these inputs drive a real-time force estimation model and a vibrotactile haptic feedback system that alerts the cardiologist when force exceeds safe thresholds.</p>
    <div class="highlight-box"><p>Key achievement: Force sensing in the 0.02–0.4 N range with under 50 ms end-to-end feedback latency — within the clinical safe force window of 0.1–0.4 N.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div class="proj-section">
    <div class="proj-section-title">Key Specifications</div>
    <div class="specs-grid">
      <div class="spec-cell"><div class="spec-label">Catheter Size</div><div class="spec-value">6 Fr</div></div>
      <div class="spec-cell"><div class="spec-label">Force Range</div><div class="spec-value">0.02 – 0.4 N</div></div>
      <div class="spec-cell"><div class="spec-label">Feedback Latency</div><div class="spec-value">&lt; 50 ms</div></div>
      <div class="spec-cell"><div class="spec-label">Safe Force Target</div><div class="spec-value">0.1 – 0.4 N</div></div>
      <div class="spec-cell"><div class="spec-label">Tip Material</div><div class="spec-value">CNT-doped elastic resin</div></div>
      <div class="spec-cell"><div class="spec-label">Shaft</div><div class="spec-value">Pebax braided + Nitinol flexure</div></div>
      <div class="spec-cell"><div class="spec-label">MCU</div><div class="spec-value">Arduino Nano 33 BLE</div></div>
      <div class="spec-cell"><div class="spec-label">Feedback Type</div><div class="spec-value">Vibrotactile (3V coin motors)</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="proj-section">
    <div class="proj-section-title">Background &amp; Clinical Motivation</div>
    <p>Cardiac ablation treats arrhythmias by creating targeted scar tissue to block faulty electrical signals. The catheter is guided through vessels to the heart under X-ray imaging and positioned at the arrhythmia site. Current commercial systems rely heavily on physician tactile intuition or expensive fibre-optic force sensing modules (e.g., Biosense Webster THERMOCOOL SMARTTOUCH). These are inaccessible for training or research environments and provide no graded haptic feedback to the operator.</p>
    <p>CNT-doped piezoresistive materials offer a biocompatible, low-cost alternative: when mechanical strain is applied, the nanotube network deforms, altering the bulk electrical resistance of the composite in a measurable and reproducible way. This project evaluated the feasibility of embedding this property directly into the catheter tip.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="proj-section">
    <div class="proj-section-title">Design &amp; Methodology</div>
    <p><strong>Tip fabrication:</strong> The distal tip was designed in SolidWorks and printed via SLA 3D printing using a flexible elastic resin. CNTs were embedded using a solvent-based doping procedure to impart piezoresistive properties while maintaining biocompatibility per ISO 10993 guidelines.</p>
    <p><strong>Force model:</strong> Contact force F is estimated as a function of three inputs — tip deformation d (resistance change via gage factor), contact angle θ (IMU), and contact velocity u (IMU). A regression model was trained on simulation data generated from a 2D FEA model of the tip contacting tissue-like materials at varying angles and speeds.</p>
    <p><strong>Haptic feedback:</strong> The Arduino Nano 33 BLE processes incoming sensor data and drives 3V vibrotactile coin motors embedded in the catheter handle. Graded vibration intensity maps to force magnitude, alerting the operator before force exceeds safe limits.</p>
    <p><strong>Material comparison:</strong> Three materials were evaluated via FEA — medical-grade silicone, TPU, and Pebax-based elastomer — across different contact scenarios using ANSYS Structural to determine the optimal compliance-durability balance.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="proj-section">
    <div class="proj-section-title">Results</div>
    <p>The CNT-doped tip demonstrated consistent, reproducible resistance changes within the target force range of 0.02–0.4 N. The MATLAB-based real-time estimation pipeline achieved end-to-end latency below 50 ms from force onset to haptic alert. FEA simulations confirmed stress distributions remained within safe tissue-contact limits for the Pebax-based elastomer tip geometry.</p>
    <p>The human-controlled closed-loop workflow — measure force → alert via haptics → cardiologist adjusts position → new force measured — was validated in bench testing using synthetic tissue phantoms.</p>
  </div>

